From 4a3abac084d4f86c1d46872a5ddc6872b72e1d69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 11:01:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 103/104] Attribute every boxing conversion to the compiler
 site that mints it

Item 3-1's storage half was re-opened by 4.2a on a conversion count that
had tripled once the census stopped running 7 of 15 suites, and whose
dominant producer had changed identity. The counter that produced that
finding sits in the boxing factory: it can say a raw double crossed into
a JSValue, and not which of the compiler's emission sites did it. Item
0113 then settled the storage question from the other side by counting
the read/write ratio, but nothing had ever said where the conversions
themselves come from.

So each of the compiler's 21 boxing sites now declares itself and the
census reports the split. The site travels as a constant argument on the
one code path the engine ships, rather than as a factory entry per site
or an argument gated behind the counter flag, which would leave the
measured arm and the shipping arm running different code.

Counted over all 15 suites: 61.8% of the corpus's 69.3 M conversions are
the guarded tree's ROOT box, and the generic fallback arm it can leak to
is 226 of 69.3 M. The tree is not leaking; what is left is the one box
per evaluation the design keeps.

Two defects found on the way. NumberBoxingDiagnostics.Reset did not
clear item 3-8a's speculative-read counter, so a host resetting between
suites carried it forward and every suite but the first read high --
which is exactly the shape a per-suite census produces. And the census
runs its suites in one process in a fixed order, so Mandreel taking the
process down cost every suite after it; the suites are now selectable,
and Gameboy, Typescript, Box2D, zlib and CodeLoad have rows for the
first time.

Verified by 7 cases that prove the instrument discriminates on
constructed shapes before any corpus number is read from it, the load
bearing one being that the eight sites partition the factory's own
independent conversion total.
